---
import AlterBase from '../layouts/AlterBase.astro';

// What the app actually sends was checked against the code (Alter 1.0.9,
// alttext.org API) on 2026-10-17. Keep it true: if Alter starts talking to
// a new service, add it under "When Alter goes online".
const lastUpdated = 'October 17, 2026';
---

<AlterBase
  title="Privacy · Alter"
  description="How Alter handles your photos and data. Short version: your library stays on your Mac. No account, no analytics, no ads, no tracking."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</p>

    <p>
      Alter is a photo library for the Mac, made by Twilightscapes, the team
      behind <a href="https://alttext.org">ALTtext.org</a>. The short version:
      <strong>your photos and your library stay on your Mac.</strong> Alter has
      no account, no analytics, no crash reporting, no ads and no tracking.
      It only goes online for the things listed below, and only when you use
      them.
    </p>

    <h2>On your Mac</h2>
    <p>
      Your library, your photos, your edits, keywords, ratings, albums and
      locations are stored in your library on your Mac (or wherever you keep
      it). We never receive them.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Faces.</strong> Face recognition runs entirely on your Mac. Alter
        turns each face into a set of numbers to find the same person in other
        photos. The numbers, and the names you give people, stay in your library
        and are never sent anywhere.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Select.</strong> The Select tool's object finding runs on your
        Mac too. No photo leaves it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sign-ins.</strong> If you connect Bluesky, the sign-in is kept in
        your Mac's Keychain.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When Alter goes online</h2>

    <h3>ALT text</h3>
    <p>
      When you ask Alter to write ALT text, it sends a smaller copy of that photo
      to <a href="https://alttext.org">alttext.org</a>. For a video, it sends up
      to eight still frames, plus a transcript of the speech in it made on your
      Mac. alttext.org passes them to <strong>Google's Gemini API</strong> and
      returns the description. We don't store the photo, the frames, the
      transcript or the description. Our Gemini account is a paid one, so
      Google doesn't use what we send to train its models.
    </p>
    <p>
      To keep the service fair, alttext.org keeps a count of how many
      descriptions are written each day. It's kept against your network
      address, or against your ALTtext key if you have Pro. The counts
      contain no photos and no text.
    </p>

    <h3>Buying Alter Pro</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>From the Mac App Store:</strong> Apple handles the payment, and we
      never see your name, Apple Account or payment details. Unlimited ALT text
      comes with Pro, so once after you buy, Alter sends Apple's signed record of
      that purchase to alttext.org, which checks it with Apple and issues an
      ALTtext key. We keep that purchase's transaction number together with the
      key, so the same purchase always gets the same key back.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>From altermedia.app:</strong> payment is handled by Stripe, as
      described in the <a href="https://alttext.org/privacy">ALTtext privacy
      policy</a>. We keep the key we issue, the email address you gave and
      Stripe's customer reference.
    </p>

    <h3>Maps and place names</h3>
    <p>
      Maps, and the place names shown for your photos, come from Apple's map
      services. To name a place, Alter sends only its location, rounded to about
      a kilometre, and never a photo. This is covered by Apple's privacy policy.
      You can turn place names off in Settings (Look up place names).
    </p>

    <h3>Downloads</h3>
    <p>
      The first time you use Faces or Select, Alter downloads the model it needs
      from Hugging Face. Alter Pro's font picker downloads fonts from Google
      Fonts. These are plain downloads. Nothing of yours is sent.
    </p>

    <h3>Places you post to</h3>
    <p>
      If you connect Bluesky, post a video, or publish a web gallery to GitHub,
      Alter sends what you chose to post to that service, under your own
      account. The sign-in happens on that service's own page. Subscribing a
      gallery on your Apple TV saves its address in your own iCloud, for the
      Twilightscapes app.
    </p>

    <h3>Feedback</h3>
    <p>
      If you send feedback from altermedia.app, we keep what you wrote, the
      Alter version if you gave it, and your email address if you left one so
      we can reply.
    </p>

    <h2>Who else is involved</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Google</strong> writes ALT text descriptions (Gemini API).</li>
      <li><strong>Apple</strong> handles App Store purchases and map services.</li>
      <li><strong>Netlify</strong> hosts altermedia.app and alttext.org, and stores the usage counts, keys and feedback.</li>
      <li><strong>Stripe</strong> handles purchases made on altermedia.app.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We don't sell your data, and we don't use advertising or tracking of any kind.</p>

    <h2>Your choices</h2>
    <p>
      Alter works fully offline apart from the features above. To ask about, or
      delete, the key and purchase record that go with your Alter Pro, email{' '}
      <a href="mailto:support@alttext.org">support@alttext.org</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>Alter isn't directed to children under 13, and we don't knowingly collect their data.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      When this page changes, the date at the top changes too, and anything
      important will be noted on the site.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions? <a href="mailto:support@alttext.org">support@alttext.org</a>.</p>
  </article>
</AlterBase>
